# Testing Needed | Updates and Fixes

File a task into the shared list below **only when the change is something a
person could click through and verify in the app**. Do this silently at the end
of the change — mention in one sentence that you filed it.

## ✅ File a task when…

- **A bug was fixed** — something was broken and now it should work; someone
  needs to confirm it does.
- **A user-visible UI change was made** — new button, label, modal, panel,
  column, badge, form field, etc. The user attached screenshots, or the change
  alters what appears on screen.
- **A workflow changed** — the sequence of steps a user takes (wizard flow,
  submit flow, payroll run, export, etc.) was modified.
- **Data a user reads changed** — a report, export CSV, pay stub, ADP output,
  or similar artifact now looks different.
- **A new user-facing feature landed** — something the user explicitly asked to
  build that they will interact with directly.

## ❌ Do NOT file a task when…

- The change is **backend/infrastructure only** with no user-visible surface —
  e.g. a new notification batching algorithm, a migration adding a column, a
  locking strategy, a refactor, a service method, or a route handler that
  isn't exposed in any new UI yet.
- The change is a **pure code quality / bug-prevention improvement** that a
  user can't tell happened (race condition guard, duplicate prevention logic,
  index added, etc.).
- The change is **already covered by another task** filed in the same session.
- The task would have no meaningful "Please test:" steps — if you can't write
  at least two concrete click-through steps, don't file it.

**Already exists — no new migration needed.** The task/project system
(`task_projects`, `task_lists`, `task_list_members`, `tasks`, `task_links`)
already supports everything required. Do not create new tables/migrations for
this.

## Where it goes

- Project: **PlotTwistHQ Development** — `task_projects.id = 3`
- Shared list: **Testing Needed | Updates and Fixes** — `task_lists.id = 10`
- Agency: ITSCO — `agency_id = 2`
- Members: all ITSCO admin/super_admin users (already added to both the
  project and list as `editor`). If a new admin/super_admin joins ITSCO later,
  add them too via `TaskListMember.add(10, userId, 'editor')` and
  `TaskProject.addMember(3, userId, 'editor')`.

## How to file the task

Use the `Task` and `TaskLink` models directly (fastest — a one-off Node
script via the Shell tool, like `node -e "..."` importing
`backend/src/models/Task.model.js` and `TaskLink.model.js`), or the
`/tasks` and `/tasks/:id/links` API routes if already authenticated in
context.

```js
import { inferTaskCategoryFromTitle, normalizeTaskCategories } from '../constants/taskCategories.js';

const title = 'Test: <short description of what changed>';
const categories = normalizeTaskCategories(inferTaskCategoryFromTitle(title));

const task = await Task.create({
  taskType: 'custom',
  title,
  description: `What was happening: ...\n\nWhy it happened: ...\n\nWhat was fixed: ...\n\nPlease test: <specific steps to verify in the app>`,
  assignedByUserId: 501, // Michael Mendez, unless another user is clearly the requester
  taskListId: 10,
  projectId: 3,
  urgency: 'medium',
  isPrivate: false,
  categories
});
await TaskLink.create({
  taskId: task.id,
  url: 'https://plottwisthq.com/<path to the exact page that changed>',
  label: 'Short page name',
  createdByUserId: 501
});
```

- `description` should mirror exactly what you told the user in chat: what was
  happening, why, what was fixed, and concrete steps to test it.
- Always attach a `task_link` with the production URL (`https://plottwisthq.com/...`)
  of the page most directly affected, when one exists.
- **Always set `categories`** when filing (infer from the title via
  `inferTaskCategoryFromTitle` — e.g. workspace tasks → `tasks_hub`, layout →
  `ui_ux`, otherwise `qa_testing` for `Test:` titles). `Task.create` will infer
  automatically if omitted, but prefer passing explicitly in the script.
- One task per distinct fix — don't bundle unrelated fixes into one task.

## On request: "log everything from this chat"

If the user asks you to summarize a chat/session and file tasks for
everything fixed in it, walk back through the conversation, identify each
distinct fix/feature, and file one task per fix using the steps above.
